I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build a multiphase GSAS-II refinement, and validate that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project are mutually consistent. I’ll treat phase count as an inference rather than an input and flag any ambiguity in the report.

The chemistry points first to cobalt tungstate (CoWO₄), but residual CoO/WO₃ or a cobalt-rich tungsten oxide are plausible at 900 °C. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed crystallographic databases/tooling so that the phase call is driven by the full pattern, not chemistry alone.

The dominant peak set is already consistent with wolframite-type CoWO₄: the characteristic cluster near 23–24.5°, followed by lines near 30.7°, 34.2°, 36.4°, and 41.5°. I’m checking every weaker line and the high-angle Kα doublet behavior next; that is what will decide whether a second crystalline phase is actually warranted.

Two phases are now required by independent peak families: monoclinic γ-WO₃ explains the 23.18/23.67/24.42° triplet and its split higher-angle lines, while wolframite CoWO₄ explains the otherwise unaccounted 19.09°, 30.71°, 36.44°, and related reflections. CoO and Co₃O₄ have no clear unique lines at their expected strongest positions. I’m moving to quantitative two-phase Rietveld refinement and will explicitly test whether adding either oxide is statistically and physically justified.

The GSAS-II environment lacks its optional automatic-background package, so I’m replacing that one convenience step with the same standard asymmetric penalized least-squares baseline implemented locally. This does not change the diffraction model or refinement engine; it only supplies a smooth starting background for the measured counts.

The first quantitative refinement gives about 70 wt% WO₃ and 30 wt% CoWO₄ with Rwp ≈ 7.25%, and both phase cells converge to plausible literature values. The phase assignment is strong, but the fully free instrumental profile is over-correlated and has nonphysical individual coefficients because no calibrated profile was supplied. I’m tightening that part of the model before accepting the fractions, then I’ll run the explicit CoO/Co₃O₄ inclusion tests.

The constrained two-phase model is stable near 69.5 wt% WO₃ / 30.5 wt% CoWO₄. I’m now doing conservative one-extra-parameter tests for CoO and Co₃O₄: their cells and widths are held at plausible values while only their nonnegative amounts are allowed to refine. This avoids mistaking profile flexibility for a real trace phase.

Both added-phase trials drove the CoO and Co₃O₄ scales to the nonnegative boundary (effectively zero); their strongest unique reflections are also absent. I’m therefore keeping the defensible two-phase model. Before export I’m simplifying the width model to one positive instrumental Gaussian term plus positive phase-size terms, and extending the refinement/profile export over the exact measured angular range.

